1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,072
Hi, here's my Week eight and nine update.

2
00:00:03,072 --> 00:00:10,369
My project compares three ways to keep a language model current: retrieval, few shot prompting, and LoRA fine tuning.

3
00:00:10,369 --> 00:00:17,666
Over these two weeks, I ran the GPU baselines, completed the first LoRA training run, and audited the answers.

4
00:00:17,666 --> 00:00:24,195
Let's look at the actual repository, the code behind those runs, and what the results tell us.

5
00:00:24,195 --> 00:00:27,344
This is the baseline cell from my Colab notebook.

6
00:00:27,344 --> 00:00:35,042
It calls the experiment runner with the Hugging Face backend and the same Qwen model for retrieval, few shot, and zero shot.

7
00:00:35,042 --> 00:00:40,290
That matters because changing the model between methods would make the comparison harder to interpret.

8
00:00:40,290 --> 00:00:44,839
The cell also checks that the run is complete before downloading the results.

9
00:00:44,839 --> 00:00:49,737
These are saved GPU experiments, not a new training session running during this video.

10
00:00:49,737 --> 00:00:51,278
Here's the evaluation loop.

11
00:00:51,278 --> 00:00:55,130
First, it retrieves a passage when the method needs one.

12
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:58,596
Then it builds the prompt and generates an answer.

13
00:00:58,596 --> 00:01:03,989
The important part is below: the same answer gets checked against both handbook versions.

14
00:01:03,989 --> 00:01:12,078
So I can measure what happens when yesterday's knowledge meets today's policy, without generating a different answer and changing the comparison.

15
00:01:12,078 --> 00:01:15,929
Each answer, expected value, and score is saved for inspection.

16
00:01:15,929 --> 00:01:18,797
For Week nine, I ran this training code.

17
00:01:18,797 --> 00:01:25,608
It loads the model in four bit precision and trains small LoRA adapters on the query and value projections.

18
00:01:25,608 --> 00:01:29,193
The rank is sixteen, with twenty epochs and thirteen examples.

19
00:01:29,193 --> 00:01:35,287
The training setup was already implemented; this week's milestone was completing the run and evaluating its output.

20
00:01:35,287 --> 00:01:42,098
This is a deliberately small training budget, so a weak result here doesn't mean fine tuning can never work.

21
00:01:42,098 --> 00:01:45,062
Here's the training log produced by that run.

22
00:01:45,062 --> 00:01:50,249
It used a Tesla T four and took about one hundred twenty four seconds.

23
00:01:50,249 --> 00:01:54,324
Peak allocated GPU memory was about three point three six gibibytes.

24
00:01:54,324 --> 00:01:58,399
That's memory allocated by the framework, not the entire GPU footprint.

25
00:01:58,399 --> 00:02:01,734
The log and evaluation answers are in the repository.

26
00:02:01,734 --> 00:02:06,920
The adapter itself still needs a durable backup check before I continue training it.

27
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,255
Now for the results.

28
00:02:08,255 --> 00:02:15,259
Both retrieval methods got ten out of eleven answers right initially, and eleven out of eleven after the handbook was refreshed.

29
00:02:15,259 --> 00:02:18,928
Few shot got ten out of eleven on both matching versions.

30
00:02:18,928 --> 00:02:21,930
The first LoRA adapter got five out of eleven.

31
00:02:21,930 --> 00:02:26,934
The middle column shows the drop when old answers are judged against the new policy.

32
00:02:26,934 --> 00:02:31,603
The LoRA update is still pending, so that last cell isn't a completed result.

33
00:02:31,603 --> 00:02:34,509
Here's one example from the saved answer file.

34
00:02:34,509 --> 00:02:39,958
With the updated examples, few shot should answer forty five days for the refund window.

35
00:02:39,958 --> 00:02:41,410
It still says thirty.

36
00:02:41,410 --> 00:02:49,038
Looking at individual answers helped catch scoring issues too, including an old answer that only matched a new policy by coincidence.

37
00:02:49,038 --> 00:02:53,760
That's why I kept the original outputs and a separate audited result file.

38
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:57,029
An accuracy number alone doesn't explain what went wrong.

39
00:02:57,029 --> 00:03:07,165
So the progress for these two weeks is concrete: comparable GPU baselines, a completed LoRA run, and an audited comparison backed by code and saved answers.

40
00:03:07,165 --> 00:03:12,623
Retrieval updated cleanly in this small test; the first LoRA recipe learned fewer facts.

41
00:03:12,623 --> 00:03:16,911
With only eleven synthetic facts, I can't claim a universal winner.

42
00:03:16,911 --> 00:03:24,707
Next, I'll update LoRA on version two and check both recovery and retention, then finish the project in Week eleven.
